arXiv:2510.04214v1 [cs.CL] 05 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhuoran Zhuang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ye Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xia Zeng*
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chao Luo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luhui Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yihan Chen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Fliggy Alibaba
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study deploying large language models (LLMs) as business development (BD) agents for persuasive price negotiation in online travel agencies (OTAs), where aligning traveler affordability and hotel profitability directly affects bookings, partner relationships, and access to travel. The agent must follow a Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) while conducting multi-turn persuasion, interpreting colloquial inputs, and adhering to guardrails (no over-promising, no hallucinations). Conventional post-training—supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or single-source reward optimization—overfits scripts, misses nuanced persuasive style, and fails to enforce verifiable business constraints.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We propose Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization (REPO), a reinforcement learning post-training framework that aligns an LLM with heterogeneous rewards: a preference-trained reward model (RM) for dense human alignment, an reward judge (RJ) for high-level persuasive behavior and SOP compliance, and programmatic reward functions (RF) for deterministic checks on numerics, formatting, and guardrails. A straightforward enhancement mechanism is proposed to combine the RM with RJ/RF signals to curb reward hacking and improve negotiation quality. In production-style evaluations— ~150 turns from real dialogues and ~ 225 turns from curated bad-case dialogues —REPO lifts average dialogue rating to 4.63: +1.20 over base, +0.83 over Direct Preference Optimization (DPO); +0.33 over Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), increases the share of conversations with at least one excellent reponse to 66.67% (+23.34 pp over GRPO), and achieves a 93.33% bad-case fix rate with 75.56% clean fixes, outperforming SFT, DPO, PPO, and GRPO. We also observe emergent capabilities—proactive empathy, localized reasoning, calibrated tactics—that surpass gold annotations.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04214v1/REPO.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:435/142;" width="435" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Framework of REPO for Alignment from Heterogeneous Reward Signals. Given a query (<math id="S0.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>), the Policy Model generates an output (<math id="S0.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>), which is evaluated through three reward components: the Reward Model (RM), providing a dense, human preference-aligned signal; the Reward Judge (RJ), an LLM-based evaluator scoring nuanced, high-level behaviors; and programmatic Reward Functions (RF), validating task-specific requirements with deterministic checks. These signals are combined to compute the total reward (<math id="S0.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>), used to guide learning via Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) to calculate the Advantage (<math id="S0.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>). The Value Model predicts the state value (<math id="S0.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>) to further refine the training process.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S0.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04214v1/sop.png" id="S0.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/246;" width="219" height="246" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Simplified SOP for the Price Negotiation Task.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proactive price negotiation is a key operational challenge for business development (BD) in online travel agencies (OTAs). In these systems, a BD agent must conduct multi-turn dialogues with hotel managers, aiming to balance user affordability and hotel profitability. Effective negotiation directly impacts room bookings, partner relationships, and overall access to cost-friendly travel. However, this process requires not only persuasive dialogue skills but also precise adherence to task-specific business rules (such as no over-promising or hallucinated offers). The complexity of these interactions—spanning colloquial input understanding, multi-turn reasoning, numeric validation, and procedural compliance—renders conventional task-oriented dialogue systems insufficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated significant advancements in task-oriented dialogue systems, improving upon traditional rule-based or modular approaches by introducing greater naturalness and generalization capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While existing studies primarily focus on reactive, user-initiated tasks like question answering, proactive price negotiation involves long-horizon persuasion requiring both strict adherence to business rules and nuanced decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krumdick et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This problem presents several new challenges that go beyond the capabilities of conventional post-training approaches:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negotiation complexity:</span> The agent must negotiate rates persuasively without damaging hotel partnerships and ensure a win–win outcome for both users and hoteliers. Such tasks require a nuanced understanding of context and competitor behaviors, along with human-like negotiation tactics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rogiers et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stageful process adherence and constraints:</span> Prices and discounts must follow long and complex multi-stage standard operating procedures (SOPs). Agents must infer the current negotiation stage from dialogue history and respond with appropriate actions specific to that stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verifiable numerics and guardrails:</span> Outputs must meet deterministic business requirements, such as accurate pricing, valid formats, and strict adherence to guardrail rules (e.g., no hallucinated business relationships or over-promising) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persuasive capabilities in adversarial scenarios:</span> Adversarial or edge-case scenarios, such as resistance to rate adjustments or misaligned incentives, require adaptive strategies for resolving conflicts while maintaining consistency and empathy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) offer steady improvements by aligning models to labeled examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, they inevitably struggle to generalize beyond their training data. Methods like Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) extend alignment through reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, but these approaches are limited by unstable training dynamics and vulnerability to "reward hacking" when relying on single-source signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization (REPO)</span>, a novel reinforcement-driven framework designed to align LLMs with the multi-faceted requirements of OTA price negotiations. REPO combines <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">three complementary reward sources</span>:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference-trained reward models (RM):</span> capturing dense human-aligned preferences to ensure persuasive style and human-like interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-a-judge Reward Judge (RJ):</span> evaluating high-level behaviors, such as SOP compliance, emotional value, and effectiveness in multi-turn persuasion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krumdick et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Programmatic reward functions (RF):</span> enforcing deterministic checks on numeric accuracy, task-specific formatting, and adherence to safety guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework combines these heterogeneous signals through a stability-preserving modulation strategy, enhancing the core RM signal with auxiliary feedback from RJ and RF to reduce reward hacking and ensure consistent, reliable learning. REPO further adopts practical training optimizations such as LoRA for efficient fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and avoids reference models or excessive group computations for scalability, making it feasible under real-world constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In empirical evaluations against strong baselines, including SFT, DPO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, REPO achieves state-of-the-art performance on multi-turn dialogue qualities and bad-case fix rates. Beyond top-line metrics, REPO exhibits emergent capabilities such as proactive empathy, localized reasoning, and calibrated negotiation strategies, surpassing gold-standard human annotations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An extensive study of LLMs for industrial-scale price negotiation tasks, addressing complex, long-horizon persuasion scenarios beyond conventional question-answering tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The development of <span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REPO</span>, the first framework to aggregate preference, judge, and programmatic rewards for alignment in complex task-oriented dialogues.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In-depth evaluations that demonstrate REPO’s superiority over competitive baselines, with evidence of its ability to improve negotiation effectiveness, compliance, and emergent persuasive capabilities that surpass human-annotated gold standards.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-Oriented Dialogue System and LLM Alignment</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Task-oriented dialogue (TOD) systems enable goal-directed interactions in structured settings such as booking, reservations, and service automation. Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have improved fluency, coverage, and generalization in TODs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, most prior research focuses on reactive, user-initiated tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Proactive price negotiation, where the agent must drive multi-turn dialogues to achieve strategic outcomes while adhering to strict business constraints, presents unique challenges. First, the agent must manage long-horizon persuasion strategies, combining context-based reasoning with calibrated emotional intelligence, to balance competing interests like traveler affordability and hotel profitability. Second, it must operate under rigid Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), ensuring compliance with task-specific constraints, such as avoiding over-promising or hallucinated offers. Third, adherence to verifiable business numerics and guardrails, including precise price setting and valid formats, must be ensured without sacrificing dialogue fluency and persuasiveness. While some progress has been made towards goal-driven persuasion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rogiers et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, addressing the coupling of procedural adherence, dialogical nuance, and heterogeneous constraints in complex, real-world negotiations remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional approaches to aligning LLMs for task-oriented dialogues center on supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on labeled datasets. While effective for mimicking patterns present in the data, SFT often suffers from limited generalization when tasked with scenarios outside the training data distribution. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) introduced optimization against preference-trained reward models as a way to embed nuanced human alignment into LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. However, RLHF is prone to reward hacking and instability, particularly in tasks like negotiation that require balancing multiple competing objectives over long conversational horizons. Recent alternatives like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) improve training stability via a non-RL pairwise preference objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; however, these methods still rely heavily on the quality and scope of preference data and lack mechanisms to enforce adherence to structured business rules or formatting requirements. Addressing these challenges requires new frameworks capable of seamlessly integrating expressive alignment signals with deterministic process constraints to ensure reliability in applications like proactive negotiation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Controllable text generation and Multi-Reward Aggregation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Controllable text generation (CTG) has gained attention for directing LLM outputs to conform to specific constraints, such as style, safety, sentiment, and task-specific requirements, without degrading language quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent CTG frameworks span a wide range of training-stage strategies—such as SFT, reinforcement learning, and task-adaptive fine-tuning—as well as inference-time approaches, including guided decoding and retrieval-enhanced generation. While suitable for general content generation tasks, these methods encounter significant limitations in complex domains such as OTA price negotiation. Specifically, these techniques lack a systematic mechanism to simultaneously fulfill heterogeneous constraints, such as managing multi-turn responses, enforcing deterministic guardrails, and achieving effective persuasion while maintaining fluency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Post-training alignment methods have explored diverse reward aggregation techniques. Preference-trained reward models (RMs) are widely used to score outputs based on human-labeled preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Evaluative methods such as LLM-as-a-judge frameworks use a task-specific rubric to assess high-level behaviors, such as persuasiveness, safety, and compliance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Programmatic reward functions (RFs) have also been developed to enforce deterministic properties such as numerical accuracy, formatting, or adherence to structural constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, current methods either rely on single-source signals or only combine a subset of these rewards, limiting their effectiveness on tasks where capturing behavior, process compliance, and verifiable outputs is critical. Efforts to aggregate multiple signals via direct reward addition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, multi-objective RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jafari et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or reward-agent frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have shown promise, but remain fragile to reward misalignment, unstable training, and suboptimal combination strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">REPO is the first approach to jointly combine all three signal families—preference-trained reward models (RMs), LLM-as-a-judge reward judge (RJ), and programmatic reward functions (RF)—into a unified reinforcement learning framework. The key innovation in REPO lies in its stability-preserving modulation mechanism, which enhances the primary RM signal with auxiliary feedback from RJ and RF. This allows REPO to address the challenges of OTA price negotiation by aligning the LLM to generate persuasive, process-adherent, and human-like responses. By leveraging its heterogeneous reward design, REPO achieves robust learning outcomes while mitigating common issues such as overfitting, reward hacking, and lack of compliance in multi-turn task-oriented dialogues.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Task Definition and Challenges</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proactive price negotiation in online travel agencies (OTAs) is a complex task where a business development (BD) agent interacts with hotels to adjust room pricing based on market conditions. The ultimate goal is to balance traveler affordability and hotel profitability—a critical factor influencing room bookings, customer satisfaction, and partner relationships. Achieving these outcomes requires striking a delicate balance: lowering rates to improve accessibility for travelers while ensuring hotel partners maintain profitability. The BD agent must negotiate within reasonable limits to secure win–win solutions, where reduced room rates drive higher booking volumes, improving both hotel revenues and user experiences. A Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) governs the negotiation flow; Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows a simplified representation of the multi-stage process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Deploying large language models (LLMs) as BD agents in this negotiation domain presents several challenges that highlight gaps in current task-oriented dialogue systems and alignment methods:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negotiation complexity.</span> Price negotiation requires nuanced, context-aware reasoning and persuasive interaction. The BD agent must tailor responses to address a variety of hotel behaviors, including reluctance to adjust rates and conflicting business priorities. Effective negotiation strategies involve calibrated concessions, competitor comparisons, empathetic framing, and clear communication, all while avoiding actions that could harm long-term hotel partnerships.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stageful process adherence and context awareness.</span> Negotiations involve multi-stage processes dictated by the SOP. At each stage, the BD agent must infer the current state from the dialogue history and act accordingly by selecting state-appropriate responses within specific procedural constraints. The dynamic nature of this task makes reasoning about context and procedural adherence a critical challenge.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verifiable numerics and guardrails.</span> Outputs generated by the BD agent must adhere to strict business constraints. These include accurate numeric reasoning, valid price formatting, and avoidance of unsupported claims such as unrealistic promises (e.g., guaranteed order increases) or the hallucination of business relationships. Violations of safety, formatting, or content guardrails can negatively impact trust in the OTA platform and damage hotel partnerships.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persuasive and adaptive response generation.</span> The BD agent must sustain engaging, fluent, and human-like interactions while adapting to various scenarios, including edge cases. This requires handling vague or off-process inputs, reframing counterarguments persuasively, and responding empathetically to pushback, all while maintaining alignment with the broader goals of negotiation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The OTA price negotiation task combines hard constraints (e.g., process adherence, numeric accuracy, and safety guardrails) with soft constraints (e.g., persuasive style and emotional value) to support human-like negotiations adhering to a complex Standard Operating Procedure. These negotiations occur over long conversational horizons, requiring dynamic inference of the current stage and appropriate action selection. Furthermore, the task demands adaptive responses suitable for a diverse range of scenarios, including both cooperative and adversarial interactions. These unique challenges illustrate the limitations of conventional methods like SFT and single-source reward optimization, which struggle to generalize to the multifaceted nature of this domain. These limitations drive the design of the <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REPO</span> framework, which integrates heterogeneous reward signals to support persuasive, compliant, and task-adherent LLM behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we introduce REPO, a reinforcement learning post-training framework that aligns an LLM using heterogeneous reward signals. Beyond a conventional preference-trained reward model (RM), REPO integrates two complementary channels: a reward judge (RJ), which employs an LLM-as-a-judge, and programmatic reward functions (RF). Each source primarily addresses a different facet of the task—RM provides dense, scalable human preference alignment; RJ evaluates nuanced, high-level behaviors (emotional value, persuasive style, SOP compliance); and RF delivers verifiable checks on business numerics, formatting, and guardrails. In our complex, multi-stage negotiation setting, combining all three is essential: each excels in distinct use cases, and several challenging capabilities only emerge when the model learns from multi-source signals.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward design</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix B Task challenges and REPO solutions. ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Appendix) summarizes task challenges and REPO solution reward sources; Table <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix C Prompts ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (Appendix) provides the judge prompt. We use three signals:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">- Reward model (RM): preference model trained on curated pairwise data; the primary dense signal for quality and alignment, essential for learning human BD persuasion style and tactics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">- Reward judge (RJ): LLM evaluator with a task-specific rubric scoring style, emotional value, SOP compliance, and progress; additive penalties improve consistency. It mitigates reward hacking and enables rapid iteration by updating the judge prompt without changing preference data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">- Reward function (RF): deterministic checks for business numerics, formatting, guardrails, and length; fast and auditable, supporting verifiable requirements with minimal changes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward enhancement</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the core of REPO is a straightforward, stability-preserving modulation scheme: auxiliary signals from the LLM-as-a-judge (RJ) and programmatic reward functions (RF) act as helping scores that scale the primary preference reward model (RM). We aggregate and clip the auxiliary signals, interpret them as a scaling term, and apply them multiplicatively to the RM:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}\;=\;\operatorname{clip}\!\bigl(E_{\mathrm{judge}}+E_{\mathrm{func}},\,-n,\,n\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>judge</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>func</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}\;=\;\operatorname{clip}\!\bigl(E_{\mathrm{judge}}+E_{\mathrm{func}},\,-n,\,n\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{total}}\;=\;R_{\mathrm{model}}\,\Bigl(1+\frac{E_{\mathrm{enh}}}{n}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{total}}\;=\;R_{\mathrm{model}}\,\Bigl(1+\frac{E_{\mathrm{enh}}}{n}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This sign-aware, magnitude-sensitive scaling has the following effects:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{model}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{model}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the reward is <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">amplified</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the reward is <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dampened</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{model}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{model}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the penalty is <span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reduced</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the penalty is <span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">amplified</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the special case where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{model}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{model}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains 0.
Clipping <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{enh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{enh}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-n,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-n,n]</annotation></semantics></math> provides controlled amplification with stability. We set n to 100 in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effective computation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given limited compute, REPO uses three techniques to reduce cost relative to standard PPO:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">- LoRA adapters on both policy and value networks to cut memory and speed training while maintaining performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">- No reference model or KL penalty; LoRA’s low-rank constraint supports stable updates without an explicit KL term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">- No group computation; we avoid group-based scoring/aggregation and compute rewards per trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These choices preserve stability and enable efficient training under tight resource budgets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04214v1/Model_Performance.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:941/1631;" width="941" height="1631" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Summary of Model Performance Comparison.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04214v1/negotiation_training_curve_english.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:1817/1019;" width="1817" height="1019" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Learning Curve of Negotiation Persuasion Capability.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct all experiments with the Qwen3-32B-Instruct model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We set the maximum response length to 512 tokens, batch size to 128, LoRA rank r=64 with LoRA alpha=64, learning rate to 1e-6, and warmup steps to 2. Please see the prompt in Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Prompts ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in Appendix. For PPO, we use PPO epochs=10 (number of optimization passes per update). The supervised phase trains with SFT and DPO for 10 epochs, and the RL phase trains with PPO, GRPO <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          For GRPO, we set the group size n=3. We ran experiments with and without a reference model; training with a reference model failed to learn and yielded substantially lower performance, so we report results without a reference model.</span></span></span>, and REPO for 2 epochs each. We report results from the best-performing checkpoint.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training data</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through iterative annotation with diverse expertise, we built a high<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>quality preference dataset of 6,632 samples. It includes 252 cases collected from online production by business experts; 3,178 samples annotated by language experts; 3,178 annotated by task experts (human BDs）; and 1,991 initially labeled as SFT (covering full SOP paths) and later enriched into preference data by human BDs. Examples are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix A Preference Data Examples ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation data</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two sets: (1) Online samples—30 complete production conversations (approximately 150 turns) reflecting the real distribution of hotel intents; and (2) Bad-case collection—45 conversations (approximately 225 turns) curated by business experts to cover diverse issues observed in deployment where the previous base model, Qwen2.5<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, made errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against common post-training approaches: SFT, DPO, and RL (PPO, GRPO). SFT is simple and stable but capped by supervised data quality and often struggles to generalize. DPO aligns with preference data and is reliable and compute-efficient, yet its ceiling remains data-limited—improvements mainly come from better preference datasets. RL offers a higher ceiling, enabling policies to surpass training exemplars and generalize more strongly; we use PPO and GRPO as representative, widely used algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All results are summarized in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Effective computation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which contains three subfigures: (a) overall dialogue rating, (b) percentage of excellent responses, and (c) bad-case fix rate. All evaluations in this section is conducted and agreed by a task expert, a linguist and a computer scientist.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Online Samples</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the online set using two metrics shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Effective computation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a)–(b): (i) overall dialogue rating on a 1–5 rubric 1 = unusable, 2 = major issues, 3 = minor issues, 4 = no issues, 5 = excellent; and (ii) percentage of excellent responses—per conversation, counted if the model produces at least one outstanding negotiation turn.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across both metrics, REPO decisively leads. On the dialogue-quality scale (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Effective computation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a)), REPO reaches 4.63, improving by +1.20 over the base (3.43), +0.83 over DPO (3.80), and +0.33 over GRPO (4.30). On excellent-response conversations (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Effective computation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b)), REPO attains 66.67%, a fivefold increase over the base (13.33%), roughly double DPO (33.33%), and +23.34 pp over GRPO (43.33%). SFT and DPO deliver steady gains, while vanilla PPO degrades both metrics (rating: 3.33 vs. 3.43; excellent: 10.00% vs. 13.33</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, performance improves as alignment signals and optimization become more effective: GRPO yields substantial gains, and REPO’s tri-source alignment (RM + RJ + RF with enhancement) delivers the largest uplift in both average dialogue rating and the incidence of excellent negotiation turns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Badcase
Collection</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess fixes across five outcomes (solved, solved with minor/major problem, unsolved, unsolved with major problem) and define fix rate as the sum of the three “solved” categories. Top-line fix rates: REPO, DPO, SFT 93.33%; PPO 86.66%; GRPO 71.10%. Residual unresolved rates: REPO/SFT 6.67%, DPO 6.66%, PPO 13.33%, GRPO 28.88%. Severe unresolved cases occur for GRPO (4.44%) and DPO (2.22%), and are absent for REPO, PPO, and SFT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Within fixed cases, clean-fix shares are highest for REPO (75.56%), then GRPO (44.44%), DPO (40.00%), PPO (33.33%), SFT (31.11%). Major-problem fixes are most common for SFT (42.22%), DPO (31.11%), PPO (31.11%), lower for GRPO (13.33%) and REPO (4.44%). Minor-problem fixes: PPO and DPO (22.22%), SFT (20.00%), REPO and GRPO (13.33%). Overall, several methods recover many bad cases, but REPO yields markedly more clean fixes and fewer problematic ones; DPO and SFT trade coverage for fix quality, and GRPO shows lower coverage with higher residual error.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04214v1/heatmap.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:848/1329;" width="848" height="1329" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Good-case Rate Heatmap on Fine-grained Conversational Skills.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Analysis and Discussion</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Emergent Negotiation Capabilities During Training</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We track persuasion ability per checkpoint using 20 randomly sampled online dialogues scored by Deepseek-R1, removing the highest/lowest score before averaging. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Effective computation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> plots the Overall Negotiation Persuasion Score over steps with smoothed fits and three heuristic phases: Initial (0–30), Learning (30–100), and Convergence (100–190). Please see the prompt in Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C Prompts ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The curve is non-monotonic but clearly upward. Early updates show instability typical of on-policy exploration; during Learning, scores rise steadily as tactics emerge (structured offers, calibrated concessions, justification chaining); by Convergence, performance plateaus with small oscillations. Late checkpoints outperform early ones by roughly +14 points (approximately 30%), and the smoothed fits confirm a sustained upward shift despite local dips. These phases capture the transition from exploration to skill formation and, finally, to a stable plateau. See Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D Examples of Emergent Negotiation Skills ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> for examples of emergent persuasive behavior during training: model responses exhibit remarkably stronger negotiation skills than the ground-truth training data. See concrete examples of emergent negotiations skills in Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D Examples of Emergent Negotiation Skills ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Fine-grained Conversational Skills Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess four binary skills with a GPT-based rater on two sets (Online Samples and Badcase Collection): dialogue fluency, workflow compliance, negotiation effectiveness, and scope understanding, reporting good-case rates per adapter in Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Badcase Collection ‣ 6 Results ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. See the assessment prompts in Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix C Prompts ‣ Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (Appendix). On Online Samples, scope adherence and workflow discipline are near-ceiling for stronger methods; fluency is generally high with moderate spread; the main separator is negotiation effectiveness, where REPO clearly leads—indicating gains in persuasive reasoning beyond stylistic or procedural improvements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the adversarial Badcase set, scores drop but rankings persist. REPO remains strongest on negotiation effectiveness, leading the next best by 16 pp; workflow compliance stays robust and scope understanding remains high, especially for REPO. Averaging across skills, REPO leads on both sets. The lower absolute level of negotiation effectiveness on Badcase (low-60s even for the best model) reflects distribution shift: this set is curated to stress rare, contentious scenarios (e.g., prior negative collaborations and complaints) where the BD must balance persuasion with empathy and relationship preservation. Consequently, the achievable persuasiveness ceiling is inherently lower than in typical production dialogues.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present REPO, a reinforcement-driven alignment approach with carefully designed, multi-source rewards that enables proactive price negotiation. In human expert evaluations—the basis of our main results—REPO consistently outperforms SFT, DPO, PPO, and GRPO across dialogue quality, incidence of excellent responses, and bad-case fixes, with higher clean-fix rates, stronger negotiation effectiveness, and solid workflow/scope compliance. LLM-based analyses of training dynamics and fine-grained skills corroborate and help explain these gains. Looking ahead, we will extend REPO to smaller backbones and broader domains and languages, with refined reward design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our focus is deploying an LLM in our business, we evaluate only the price negotiation task, so demonstrating broader effectiveness will require extensive experiments across diverse tasks and settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We declare that there are no conflicts of interest, ethical concerns, or potential risks associated with this work. All of the used scientifc artifacts are public open-source artifacts that are under licenses such as Apache License 2.0 and CC-BY 4.0 License and our use is consistent with their intended use. All used data does not contain any information that names or uniquely identifies individual people or offensive content and has been manually checked by the authors.</p>
</div>
</section>
